{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST document the user and project configuration locations, the common\nfile structure and schema version, and how to create and edit the files by hand.\nEach configurable feature MUST document its fields, defaults, resolution rules,\nand credential-source references, with complete, copyable examples identifying\nall placeholders and the file to edit. Documentation MUST explain how to change\nor remove optional settings and selections.\n\nConfiguration MUST NOT require a configuration-management command or generated\nstate beyond the documented files. Primary examples MUST use credential-source\nreferences; examples explaining explicit credential fields MUST use clearly\nidentified placeholders, never real secrets.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover each obligation in `requirements.configuration.documentation`. I found no gap.\n\n**Locations, structure, schema version, hand editing.**\n- `tests/configuration.rs#fn[configuration_documentation_covers_locations_structure_editing_and_removal]` reads `md/reference.md` and asserts specific phrases.\n- Those phrases cover the user locations (`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, `$HOME/.config`, `%APPDATA%`), the nearest enclosing `habeas.toml`, `version = 1`, and the rule that the top level holds only feature tables.\n- They also cover hand creation and editing (\"no configuration-management command\", \"never creates, repairs, or rewrites\").\n\n**Per-feature fields, defaults, resolution rules, credential-source references.**\n- The same test asserts the following:\n  - Selection order.\n  - Evidence allow/disallow semantics, including the empty-list default.\n  - The `reauthorings` default of 3.\n  - The usage-record and GitHub state locations.\n  - `api_key_env` and `aws_profile` references.\n- `tests/configuration.rs#fn[authoring_documentation_shows_each_configuration_task]` adds the authoring chapter and the following checks:\n  - Resolution rules (nearest file, override as a whole, precedence of selections).\n  - Defaults for concurrency, policy, model, endpoint, and Bedrock tokens.\n  - Backend options.\n  - Credential-source guidance for OpenAI, Ollama, and Bedrock.\n\n**Complete, copyable examples naming the file and placeholders.**\n- `tests/configuration.rs#fn[documented_configuration_examples_are_complete_valid_files]` parses every TOML block in the three documents with `UserConfig::parse` or `WorkspaceConfig::parse`. It also requires `version = 1` as the first line.\n- `tests/configuration.rs#fn[documented_configuration_examples_name_the_file_to_edit]` requires each example's introducing paragraph to name `config.toml` or `habeas.toml`. It also re-parses the example as that file kind.\n- `tests/configuration.rs#fn[documented_configuration_examples_identify_their_placeholders]` requires every placeholder (uppercase values, `research`, `us-east-1`, `workstation`, the project path, `generated`) to be identified in the surrounding prose.\n- `authoring_documentation_shows_each_configuration_task` adds per-example checks of the target file and placeholder instructions.\n\n**Changing and removing optional settings and selections.** Removal and edit phrases are asserted in both documentation tests. Examples are \"delete its key or table\", \"Remove the project table or default field\", \"delete `concurrency`\", \"Delete `endpoint`\", and \"delete the whole `[evidence]` table\".\n\n**No management command or generated state.**\n- The documentation phrases above assert this.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[authoring_uses_nearest_workspace_configuration_without_a_user_file]` checks it behaviorally. It runs authoring with only a hand-written workspace file. Neither configuration file is rewritten, and no user `config.toml` is created.\n- `absent_and_minimal_configuration_are_distinct_and_read_only` and `workspace_discovery_uses_nearest_file_without_merging_or_rewriting_it` show that loading is read-only and that absent files are not created.\n\n**Credential-source references as primary examples, placeholders for explicit credentials.**\n- The OpenAI example must use `api_key_env`.\n- `documented_configuration_examples_are_complete_valid_files` finds the literal-credential examples. It asserts there is exactly one, that it sits in the authoring chapter, and that every secret value is an all-uppercase placeholder. That rules out real-looking secrets.\n- The runtime test uses a literal key only as a test fixture and checks that it does not leak into usage records or output.\n\nContent checks use phrase matching, which is the only practical way to test documentation prose. The phrases chosen are specific enough that deleting a required topic would fail a test. All obligations are mapped to tests, and the tests are marked with `{test}` for this requirement.",
  "references": "2388d8cacfe78fd516ed8363d5fe81a57f29bca50e575d3c2674afcddad4d201",
